    "address": "Liberty Island, New York, NY 10004",
    "desc": "The Only Official Vendor\nStatue City Cruises is the only authorized ticket provider. Ignore the aggressive ticket sellers in Battery Park; they are pushing third-party harbor cruises that will not actually let you off the boat onto the island.\n\nThe Starboard Advantage\nWhen departing from Manhattan, secure a spot on the starboard (right) side of the ferry. This position gives you the absolute best unobstructed approach photos of the statue as the boat arcs toward the dock.\n\nThe Pedestal Upgrade\nA standard \"General Admission\" ticket only lets you walk the island grounds. If you want to go inside the monument's base and access the stone balconies, you must specifically select a \"Pedestal Reserve\" ticket, which has limited inventory.\n\nThe 8:30 AM Strategy\nBook the absolute first ferry of the day. Mid-day airport-style security lines at Castle Clinton in Battery Park can easily add an agonizing 60 to 90 minutes of standing in the sun before you even board the boat.\n\nThe Locker Quarter Hack\nLarge daypacks are strictly prohibited inside the monument. If you have pedestal or crown access, you must use the lockers in the security tent. They require a physical $0.25 coin deposit, and there are no change machines.\n\nTelephoto Ferry Stabilization\nIf you are shooting the approach with a heavy telephoto lens (like a 100-400mm) from the moving ferry, ensure your lens's Optical SteadyShot is engaged and keep your shutter speed above 1/1000s to completely freeze the boat's engine vibration.\n\nThe Standalone Museum\nDo not skip the new Statue of Liberty Museum located on the backside of the island. It is fully included in your general admission ticket, is highly interactive, and houses the monument's massive original 1886 copper torch.\n\nNew Jersey Departure Hack\nYou can depart from either New York or New Jersey. The Liberty State Park (NJ) departure line is notoriously shorter, has ample on-site parking, and generally moves much faster than the chaotic Manhattan side.\n\nThe Rear Viewpoint\nMost tourists crowd the front plaza upon disembarking. Walk all the way around to the back of the monument for a stunning, completely uncrowded perspective of the statue looking out toward the Manhattan skyline.\n\nStrict Ferry Sequencing\nYour ticket automatically includes Ellis Island, but ferries follow a strict one-way loop: Battery Park to Liberty, then to Ellis, then back to Manhattan. You cannot backtrack to Liberty Island once you leave it.\n\nOffline Your QR Codes\nCell service can be surprisingly spotty around the heavy stone of Castle Clinton and out on the water. Screenshot your digital QR codes before you leave your hotel so you aren't stuck waiting for an email to load at the gate.\n\nTime Budget Math\nDo not attempt to squeeze this into a two-hour window. Between the two ferry rides, dual security checkpoints, and exploring both islands, budget a strict minimum of 4.5 to 5 hours for the full experience.\n\nFood & Hydration Logistics\nThe food at the Crown Cafe on the island is basic and heavily marked up. Pack your own snacks and water in a clear container to eat on the ferry or on the island's grassy park areas.\n\nThe Free Alternative\nIf you only want water-level photos of the monument and do not actually care about stepping foot on Liberty Island, just ride the free Staten Island Ferry. It passes incredibly close and costs absolutely nothing.\n\nThe Audio Tour App\nAn excellent official audio tour is included with every single ticket. Instead of using the provided public headsets, download the official National Park Service app beforehand so you can listen seamlessly on your own earbuds.",
    "summary": "An iconic 19th-century copper statue standing guard over New York Harbor, complete with a dedicated museum and historic grounds.",
