    "address": "Liberty Island, New York, NY 10004",
    "desc": "The Six-Month Window\nCrown tickets are capped at a few hundred per day and release exactly six months in advance. Book a morning slot immediately when your date opens; they sell out in hours.\n\nThe Wristband Rule\nYour printed digital ticket is not enough for the Crown. You must retrieve a physical access wristband at the Will Call window inside Castle Clinton before boarding the ferry.\n\nThe 162-Step Reality\nThere is absolutely no elevator access past the pedestal. You must be physically capable of climbing a 162-step, highly confined double-helix spiral staircase to reach the top.\n\nThe Clearance Hazard\nThe copper ceiling drops drastically as you ascend. If you are over six feet tall, you will spend the final 50 steps hunched over; take your time and watch the exposed rivets.\n\nStrict Gear Limits\nOnly one single camera (no bags), your phone, and water in a clear container are allowed in the Crown. Everything else must go into secondary lockers at the pedestal base.\n\nThe Quarter Hack\nThe mandatory storage lockers at the secondary security checkpoint require a physical $0.25 coin deposit. There are no change machines on the island, so bring a quarter with you.\n\nThe Copper Oven\nThe interior of the statue acts like a giant copper greenhouse and is not climate-controlled. Wear breathable layers and secure, rubber-soled shoes for the metal steps.\n\nHydration Logistics\nBring your own water in a clear plastic bottle. The 377 total steps from the lobby to the Crown will easily dehydrate you, especially during summer heat spikes inside the statue.\n\nPacing the Spiral\nDon't rush the ascent. There are four small rest platforms built directly into the spiral. Use them to catch your breath and let faster climbers bypass you safely.\n\nOne-Way Traffic\nThe staircase operates on a strict one-way system. The ascent and descent spirals are entirely separate, meaning you cannot turn around once you start the final climb.\n\nThe 10-Minute Limit\nPark Rangers strictly enforce a 10-minute time limit inside the Crown itself to keep the queue moving. Absorb the view first, then spend your final minutes taking photos.\n\nThe Wide Lens Strategy\nSpace is incredibly tight, and the viewing windows are tiny. Leave bulky telephoto lenses in your locker and mount your widest lens before you start the climb. Rely on your camera's in-body stabilization for the dark staircase.\n\nDouble Security Buffer\nCrown access requires clearing two separate airport-style security checkpoints: one before the ferry, and a secondary one at the monument base. Budget an extra 45 minutes for this alone.\n\nThe Rubber Hood Trick\nThe 25 windows in the Crown are prone to heavy harbor glare. Bring a collapsible rubber lens hood to press flush against the glass for reflection-free shots of the skyline.\n\nThe 8:30 AM Advantage\nBook the very first ferry of the morning. You will beat the intense midday heat inside the statue, experience zero lines at the secondary security check, and free up your afternoon.",
    "summary": "Climb the Statue's iconic spiral staircase to reach the Crown, where you’ll enjoy a unique close-up view of the harbour and New York City skyline.",
