    "address": "20 W 34th St., New York, NY 10001",
    "desc": "Skip the Box Office\nDon't waste time at the 2nd-floor ticketing desk. Buy tickets online, have your digital QR code ready, and proceed directly to the mandatory security screening line.\n\nThe Sunset Surcharge\nStandard tickets incur a hidden surcharge if booked during the 90-minute window right before sunset. To avoid the fee, book a mid-afternoon slot and simply stay on the deck until the sun goes down.\n\nThe Dedicated Entrance\nDo not try to enter through the Fifth Avenue lobby used by office workers. The Observatory has a dedicated entrance exclusively for visitors located at 20 W 34th Street.\n\nThe 2nd Floor Exhibits\nDon't sprint through the queue. The 2nd floor features highly immersive, museum-quality exhibits detailing the building's 1930s construction and pop-culture history that are worth your time.\n\nRestrooms on the 86th\nUse the facilities on the 86th floor before you join the massive queue for the elevators back down to the lobby, as there are no public restrooms available during the descent.\n\nThe 8:00 AM Empty Deck\nTo have the 86th-floor terrace almost entirely to yourself, book the very first time slot of the day. The heavy tourist crowds generally do not start arriving until 10:30 AM.\n\nThe Golden Hour Math\nTo perfectly capture the sunset transition, book your entry slot for exactly 60 minutes before the official sunset time. This gives you a buffer for security and the elevator ride.\n\nLate Night Ascents\nThe observatory is often open until midnight or later. Visit after 10:30 PM for zero lines and a completely unobstructed view of the illuminated city grid.\n\nThe 102nd Floor Reality\nThe 102nd-floor upgrade offers slightly higher views, but it is entirely enclosed. Stick to the 86th floor if you want the classic open-air atmosphere and unobstructed photos.\n\nThe Art Deco Masterpiece\nBefore rushing to the elevators, pause in the main lobby. The 24-karat gold and aluminum leaf ceiling mural is an architectural masterpiece and one of the best photo ops in the building.\n\nThrough the Railings\nOn the 86th floor, position your camera directly in the gaps between the heavy diamond safety fencing to get perfectly clean, wide skyline shots without any foreground obstructions.\n\nHigh-Altitude Aviation Spotting\nLook south toward Newark and JFK. The 86th-floor elevation provides an incredible, unobstructed vantage point to track and photograph commercial aircraft banking into their landing patterns.\n\nTelephoto Compression\nIf you are packing longer glass like a 100-400mm lens, the open-air deck is perfect. Point it northeast to beautifully compress the Chrysler Building against the East River.\n\nBeating the Wind\nThe 86th-floor deck is exposed to heavy high-altitude winds. Rely on your camera's in-body stabilization and keep your shutter speeds high to ensure crisp shots when shooting handheld.\n\nThe Rubber Hood Trick\nIf you do visit the enclosed 102nd floor, bring a collapsible rubber lens hood. Press it entirely flush against the glass to completely eliminate internal glare from the exit signs and lights behind you.",
    "summary": "An iconic Manhattan skyscraper offering sweeping 360° views of New York from its famous open-air observation deck.",
