    "id": "1788894472996",
    "name": "Staple Street Skybridge",
    "category": "sightseeing",
    "day": "",
    "address": "9 Jay St, New York, NY 10013",
    "desc": "The Exact Coordinates\nDo not look for a major avenue. This hidden architectural relic spans a tiny, two-block alleyway in Tribeca, running strictly between Harrison Street and Jay Street.\n\nThe Hospital Origins\nThe bridge was not built for luxury. Constructed in 1907 by the New York Hospital, it functioned strictly to connect the third-floor surgical clinic at 9 Jay Street to the ambulance annex and horse stables across the alley.\n\nThe Private Residence\nIt is no longer a medical facility. The bridge and the connected buildings were converted into a massive, ultra-luxury private townhouse. You cannot go inside or walk across the span.\n\nThe Cobblestone Canvas\nStaple Street is one of the few remaining thoroughfares in Manhattan still fully paved with original, heavily rutted granite Belgian blocks. Avoid thin heels; wear comfortable, flat walking shoes.\n\nThe Fashion Shoot Gridlock\nThis is one of the most heavily photographed alleys in the city. Expect to actively dodge commercial fashion shoots, wedding photographers, and tourists fighting for the exact same center angle.\n\nThe Morning Void\nTo secure a clean, cinematic shot without tripods and influencers blocking the background, you must arrive before 8:00 AM on a weekday. The alley will be completely deserted.\n\nThe Vertical Squeeze\nThe street is incredibly narrow and hemmed in by tall brick buildings. Use a wide-angle lens and shoot strictly in portrait orientation to capture both the rough cobblestones and the floating bridge in a single frame.\n\nThe Jay Street Vantage\nFor the classic, perfectly symmetrical composition, stand at the intersection of Jay Street and Staple Street looking south. The bridge will perfectly frame the historic, faded brick facades at the far end of the alley.\n\nThe Cast-Iron Details\nDo not just take wide shots. Equip a telephoto lens to punch in on the bridge itself. The three-story-high span features intricate cast-iron paneling and massive arched windows that get completely lost in standard smartphone photos.\n\nThe Shadow Trap\nBecause the alley is so deep and narrow, direct sunlight almost never hits the pavement. Expose your camera specifically for the deep shadows, or shoot on an overcast day for flat, even lighting.\n\nThe Gotham Aesthetic\nReturn after dark. When the antique streetlamps illuminate the red brick walls and the alley empties out, the floating bridge looks exactly like a gritty, 19th-century Gotham City film set.\n\nThe Misleading Name\nIt is called Staple Street not because of office supplies, but because historically, this specific area was the primary port unloading zone for the city's imported staple agricultural products.\n\nThe Loading Dock Hazard\nDespite the pedestrian, storybook vibe, this is a working commercial alley for the surrounding buildings. Be prepared to quickly move your camera gear out of the way for reversing delivery trucks and garbage crews.\n\nThe Ghostbusters Pivot\nOnce you finish your photos, walk exactly two blocks north to the corner of North Moore and Varick Streets. You will immediately find Hook & Ladder Company 8, the iconic, real-life firehouse from the Ghostbusters films.\n\nThe Subway Approach\nTake the 1 train to the Franklin Street station. Emerge from the underground and walk exactly two blocks west into Tribeca for a completely flat, three-minute walk to the Jay Street entrance.",
    "summary": "Staple Street Skybridge is a historic covered bridge in Tribeca, linking former factory buildings and offering a glimpse of old New York.",
    "image": "images/95.jpg",
    "cost": 0,
    "packageId": null,
