    "name": "Mama’s TOO!",
    "category": "dining",
    "day": "",
    "address": "2750 Broadway, New York, NY 10025",
    "desc": "The Exact Coordinates\nDo not wander aimlessly around the Upper West Side. This absolute masterpiece of modern pizza engineering is located at 2750 Broadway, sitting perfectly between 105th and 106th Streets.\n\nThe Focaccia Architecture\nDo not expect a standard, foldable New York triangle. Their signature square slices are built on a highly hydrated, cold-fermented dough that bakes into a towering, incredibly airy focaccia with a brilliant, structured crunch.\n\nThe Caramelized Crown\nThe absolute best part of the square slice is the perimeter. The chefs push the premium cheese completely to the edges of the heavy steel pans, creating a deeply caramelized, beautifully crispy cheese halo known as a frico.\n\nThe Angry Nonna\nYou absolutely must order their legendary Angry Nonna slice. It is a flawless, vibrant combination of fresh house-made mozzarella, aged provolone, hot soppressata, and Calabrian chili paste, brilliantly finished with a generous drizzle of hot honey.\n\nThe Cupping Pepperoni\nLook closely at the meat on their classic square. They use highly specific, natural-casing pepperoni that reacts to the intense deck oven heat by aggressively curling up into crispy, brilliant little chalices that perfectly hold the savory rendered fat.\n\nThe Culinary Weight\nThis is a gloriously rich, olive-oil-infused culinary milestone. The towering dough and heavy application of premium cheese carry a dense, deeply satisfying weight that will easily fuel a massive day of walking the city.\n\nThe Shared Tasting\nBecause the square slices are so structurally dense and physically filling, split one square and one of their masterful thin-crust round slices with a travel companion. This allows you to beautifully sample two completely distinct dough styles in one visit.\n\nThe House-Made Mozzarella\nNotice the luxurious, milky texture of the cheese. They do not rely on standard commercial blocks; the kitchen meticulously stretches and pulls their own fresh mozzarella daily to ensure an absolutely flawless melt.\n\nThe Reheat Mastery\nWatch the counter staff carefully. Every slice is brilliantly rejuvenated in the roaring hot deck ovens right before serving, ensuring that the bottom crust regains an absolutely flawless, crackling crunch on every single bite.\n\nThe Bleecker Street Expansion\nIf your itinerary keeps you strictly in the West Village, do not worry. They recently opened a stunning, highly acclaimed second location at 320 Bleecker Street, offering the exact same flawless quality in a beautiful downtown setting.\n\nThe Macro Detail\nBefore taking a bite, activate your camera's dedicated macro mode. Get incredibly close to capture the beautiful, honeycomb-like air pockets in the dough and the glistening pools of hot honey.\n\nThe Golden Hour Glow\nTake your beautiful slices to the outdoor seating area. Visit exactly at golden hour to let the warm, natural afternoon light bounce off the Broadway architecture and flawlessly illuminate the vibrant red tomatoes and shining pepperoni.\n\nThe Street Backdrop\nDo not just photograph the slice looking straight down. Hold the pizza up and use a wide aperture to brilliantly frame the towering, colorful slice against the classic, bustling pre-war architecture of the Upper West Side.\n\nThe Riverside Park Pivot\nOnce you finish your incredibly satisfying meal, take a beautiful, scenic walk to burn off the heavy carbohydrates. Head exactly three blocks west to seamlessly enter the beautifully landscaped, sweeping, tree-lined paths of Riverside Park.\n\nThe Subway Approach\nTransit is incredibly effortless. Take the 1 train directly to the 103rd Street station. Emerge from the underground and enjoy a beautiful, incredibly scenic three-block walk north along the vibrant Broadway corridor to hit the storefront.",
    "summary": "Mama’s TOO! is known for bold, creative pizza with crispy square slices, rich toppings, and specialties like Angry Nonna and Cacio e Pepe.",
